ion in Gale Crater.\n<p>This was the first sunset observed in color by Curiosity. The image comes from the left-eye camera of the rover's Mast Camera (Mastcam). The color has been calibrated and white-balanced to remove camera artifacts.\nMission: Mars Science Laboratory (MSL)\nTarget: Mars\nSpacecraft: Curiosity\n</p>\nInstrument: Mastcam","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2015-04-15","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://www.jpl.nasa.gov/spaceimages/details.php?id=pia19400\" href=\"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/spaceimages/details.php?id=pia19400\">http://www.jpl.nasa.gov/spaceimages/details.php?id=pia19400</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"Mars Science Laboratory (MSL), Curiosity, MastCam, NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS/Texas A&amp;M Univ.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"false","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Copyrighted":{"value":"False","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"pd","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}}}]},"40074558":{"pageid":40074558,"ns":6,"title":"File:PIA19400-MarsCuriosityRover-GaleCrater-Sunset-20150415.jpg","index":1,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":47634,"width":1344,"height":1080,"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/PIA19400-MarsCuriosityRover-GaleCrater-Sunset-20150415.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PIA19400-MarsCuriosityRover-GaleCrater-Sunset-20150415.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=40074558","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2015-05-09 01:26:42","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"PIA19400-MarsCuriosityRover-GaleCrater-Sunset-20150415","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"PD NASA|Files with no machine-readable license|Gale Crater|Widescreen desktop backgrounds|Photos by the Curiosity rover Mastcam|Sunsets on Mars|Astronomical images from California Institute of Technology","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"PIA19400: Sunset in Mars' Gale Crater \n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA19400\" href=\"https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA19400\">http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA19400</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/archive/PIA19401.gif\" href=\"https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/archive/PIA19401.gif\">Animated GIF version</a>\n</p><p>NASA's Curiosity Mars rover recorded this view of the sun setting at the close of the mission's 956th Martian day, or sol (April 15, 2015), from the rover's location in Gale Crater.\n</p><p>This was the first sunset observed in color by Curiosity. The image comes from the left-eye camera of the rover's Mast Camera (Mastcam). The color has been calibrated and white-balanced to remove camera artifacts. Mastcam sees color very similarly to what human eyes see, although it is actually a little less sensitive to blue than people are.\n</p><p>Dust in the Martian atmosphere has fine particles that permit blue light to penetrate the atmosphere more efficiently than longer-wavelength colors. That causes the blue colors in the mixed light coming from the sun to stay closer to sun's part of the sky, compared to the wider scattering of yellow and red colors. The effect is most pronounced near sunset, when light from the sun passes through a longer path in the atmosphere than it does at mid-day.\n</p><p>Malin Space Science Systems, San Diego, built and operates the rover's Mastcam. NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory, a division of the California Institute of Technology, Pasadena, manages the Mars Science Laboratory Project for NASA's Science Mission Directorate, Washington. JPL designed and built the project's Curiosity rover.\n</p>\nFor more information about Curiosity, visit <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/msl\" href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/msl\">http://www.nasa.gov/msl</a> and <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://mars.jpl.nasa.gov/msl\" href=\"https://mars.jpl.nasa.gov/msl\">http://mars.jpl.nasa.gov/msl</a>.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2015-04-15","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/jpeg/PIA19400.jpg\" href=\"https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/jpeg/PIA19400.jpg\">http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/jpeg/PIA19400.jpg</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"40074641":{"pageid":40074641,"ns":6,"title":"File:PIA19401-MarsCuriosityRover-GaleCrater-Sunset-Animation-20150415.gif","index":4,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":2222373,"width":1344,"height":1080,"duration":2,"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/85/PIA19401-MarsCuriosityRover-GaleCrater-Sunset-Animation-20150415.gif?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PIA19401-MarsCuriosityRover-GaleCrater-Sunset-Animation-20150415.gif","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=40074641","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2015-05-09 01:37:25","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"PIA19401-MarsCuriosityRover-GaleCrater-Sunset-Animation-20150415","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"PD NASA|Curiosity rover|Files with no machine-readable license|Animated GIF files|Gale Crater|Animations from NASA|Sunsets on Mars|Astronomical images from California Institute of Technology","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"PIA19401: Sunset Sequence in Mars' Gale Crater (Animation)\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA19401\" href=\"https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA19401\">http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/catalog/PIA19401</a>\n</p><p>This frame is from sequence of views NASA's Curiosity Mars rover recorded of the sun setting at the close of the mission's 956th Martian day, or sol (April 15, 2015), from the rover's location in Gale Crater.\n</p><p>Click here for larger version of <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/archive/PIA19401.gif\" href=\"https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/archive/PIA19401.gif\">PIA189401</a>\n</p><p>NASA's Curiosity Mars rover recorded this sequence of views of the sun setting at the close of the mission's 956th Martian day, or sol (April 15, 2015), from the rover's location in Gale Crater.\n</p><p>The four images shown in sequence here were taken over a span of 6 minutes, 51 seconds.\n</p><p>This was the first sunset observed in color by Curiosity. The images come from the left-eye camera of the rover's Mast Camera (Mastcam). The color has been calibrated and white-balanced to remove camera artifacts. Mastcam sees color very similarly to what human eyes see, although it is actually a little less sensitive to blue than people are.\n</p><p>Dust in the Martian atmosphere has fine particles that permit blue light to penetrate the atmosphere more efficiently than longer-wavelength colors. That causes the blue colors in the mixed light coming from the sun to stay closer to sun's part of the sky, compared to the wider scattering of yellow and red colors. The effect is most pronounced near sunset, when light from the sun passes through a longer path in the atmosphere than it does at mid-day.\n</p><p>Malin Space Science Systems, San Diego, built and operates the rover's Mastcam. NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory, a division of the California Institute of Technology, Pasadena, manages the Mars Science Laboratory Project for NASA's Science Mission Directorate, Washington. JPL designed and built the project's Curiosity rover.\n</p>\nFor more information about Curiosity, visit <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/msl\" href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/msl\">http://www.nasa.gov/msl</a> and <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://mars.jpl.nasa.gov/msl\" href=\"https://mars.jpl.nasa.gov/msl\">http://mars.jpl.nasa.gov/msl</a>.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2015-04-15","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/archive/PIA19401.gif\" href=\"https://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/archive/PIA19401.gif\">http://photojournal.jpl.nasa.gov/archive/PIA19401.gif</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"64293539":{"pageid":64293539,"ns":6,"title":"File:Pia19400-16.jpg","index":2,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":72002,"width":1344,"height":529,"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Pia19